## Agent Usage
- Make sure to spawn the correct agent for the task
- **NO SUBAGENT-TO-SUBAGENT SPAWNING, EVER.** Only the top-level orchestrator (the main conversation with the user) may spawn agents — this is enforced at the tool level (no specialist agent's `tools:` list includes the Agent/Task tool), so it cannot happen by accident. If you ARE a specialist subagent and hit a task outside your own scope/tool access, do not attempt to spawn anything — you can't — just stop and clearly report back to the orchestrator exactly what out-of-scope step is needed and why, so the orchestrator can spawn the right agent next.
- **Orchestrator: avoid redundant re-spawns.** Because subagents can't call each other, they'll sometimes report back "I still need X" (e.g. venv setup, port allocation) more than once across a multi-step task — don't treat every such report as a fresh request. Before re-spawning system-devops-admin (or any agent) for something that sounds routine, check whether it was already handled earlier in this same conversation (e.g. check `docs/system-devops-admin.md`, check if `venv/` already exists) rather than spawning again on reflex. A flat call tree only stays easy to audit if the orchestrator also avoids duplicate round-trips.
- When request involves BACKEND coding (python, node, go, etc.): use **backend-coder**
- When request involves UI (HTML/CSS/JS/templates/components): use **frontend-brand-guardian**
- For backend routes always use **backend-coder**
- After backend + frontend work on the same feature: run **frontend-backend-sync-reviewer** to verify the API contract matches on both sides
- For scheduled tasks, crons, workers: always spawn **scheduled-tasks-coder**
- For any database schema change (tables, columns, constraints, indexes, migrations): use **database-schema-manager**
- For system-level work (packages, nginx, SSL, ports, systemd, sudo): use **system-devops-admin**
- For designing/modifying multi-step LLM pipelines: use **llm-workflow-architect**
- For exploring HTTP/API surfaces of web apps (HEADLESS ONLY): use **headless-http-explorer**
- For visual UI debugging with a real browser (optional agent, needs setup): use **ui-browser-debugger**
- When spawning parallel agents, if they need to know each other's business, pre-generate that and send it to both for full sync. Example: when building a new endpoint, decide the route path + payload shape up front and include it in BOTH the backend-coder and frontend-brand-guardian prompts.

