## Project Documentation
- Agents maintain their own documentation independently — avoid creating documentation with the general-purpose agent unless directly asked by the user. Never assume documentation is needed; let agents do their thing.
- docs/ folder is worth checking at the start of any task
- Devops information: docs/system-devops-admin.md
- Backend routes: docs/backend-routes.md (backend-coder maintains this)
- Scheduled tasks: docs/scheduled-tasks.md (scheduled-tasks-coder maintains this)
- Database schema: docs/database-schema-manager.md (database-schema-manager maintains this)
- Brand guidelines: docs/brandbook.md (frontend-brand-guardian maintains this)
- LLM pipelines: docs/workflows.md (llm-workflow-architect maintains this)
- HTTP/API surface maps: docs/headless-http.md (headless-http-explorer maintains this)

## Development Practices
- **Verify against the authoritative source, not a rendered view or a generic probe.** When confirming a change is actually live/working, diff the served output against the file on disk (hash/byte comparison), not just a screenshot or page reload — template caches, stale worker processes, and browser caching can all make a correct change look like it didn't take. Same for permission checks: test the EXACT command that will actually run, not a generic stand-in.
- Prefer CURL over test scripts when possible; archive test scripts after use
- Python projects need an isolated venv so apps don't clash on a shared server. This is the orchestrator's job to arrange (subagents never spawn devops themselves): before/alongside any Python work, check whether `venv/` already exists in the project; only spawn system-devops-admin for venv setup if it doesn't. Never re-spawn devops for a venv that's already provisioned.
- For flask/fastAPI or express: port allocation is the orchestrator's job via system-devops-admin, decided once per project up front — never decided ad hoc by a coding subagent, and never re-requested once a port is already assigned (check docs/system-devops-admin.md first).
- Documentation: NEVER create documentation files on your own (agents handle this), unless the user specifically requests it
- Scripts: temporary/debug/analysis/test scripts go in playground/ in the project root (create if not existing)
- Console logs: ask devops to use journalctl for viewing, filtering, or monitoring
